<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#imperial-preceptor">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imperial Preceptor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1260">1260</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Dishi, State Preceptor, Guoshi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Imperial Preceptor, or Dishi, 'Teacher of the Emperor', was a high title and powerful post of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> as part of the Mongol patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> and Yuan rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. In 1260 Kublai named the Sakya lama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogon-chogyal-phagpa">Drogön Chögyal Phagpa</a> State Preceptor (Guoshi) and put him in charge of all Buddhist clergy; when the title was renamed in 1270, Phagpa became Imperial Preceptor. A Sakya monk residing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> held the post, supervising the clergy of the empire and advising on appointments in Tibet. After the Ming overthrew the Yuan in 1368, the title was revoked and replaced by titles of lesser status.</p></div>
